  "summary": "Distinctive Islamic architectural style developed by the Mamluk dynasty in Egypt and Syria.",
  "description": "Characterized by intricate stone-carved geometric patterns, muqarnas (stalactite vaulting), and elaborate domes. Developed as a demonstration of Mamluk power and religious devotion, often repurposing materials from earlier periods.",
  "culturalContext": "Mamluk architecture (1250-1517) — Cairo's Golden Age — built the densest concentration of medieval Islamic monuments anywhere: Sultan Hasan's four-iwan madrasa-mosque, Qalawun's hospital complex, and the domed-and-minareted funerary khanqahs lining Qarafa's \"City of the Dead\". Signature elements: ablaq striped masonry, octagonal minarets with carved balconies, red-and-cream stone joggling, crested dome profiles and blue mosaic marquetry. It expressed a military-caste patronage competitive in piety and scale until the Ottoman conquest annexed the style.",
